Dear All3DP Editors,

Solidon3D 0.3.3 has been released, and there is a simple five-minute test that
fits your practical CAD and STL coverage: import a useful model with the wrong
mounting holes, click one of them, and move it three millimetres, without
finding the source CAD file or rebuilding the part.

For a long time only the diameter could be changed. Moving a recognised feature
is what was missing, and it is there now: a hole, a peg or a dome can be moved,
rotated, resized, duplicated and removed. The
difference matters more than it sounds. Filling a hole and drilling a new one
produces the same geometry but a different feature, so every fit that referred
to it loses its reference. Moving keeps the identity, and with it the fit.

What a reader sees while doing it:

* a *Feature* panel opens on the right at the first click and shows the
  measured values as editable fields (position, diameter, depth, axis) with a
  live preview while typing;
* a checkbox applies the change to all features of the same kind, turning a row
  of four holes into one edit with a single undo;
* a hole names its standard size, "measures 5.19 mm, the clearance hole for
  M5", and says so when none fits;
* two selected features report their centre-to-centre distance;
* only what a printer can produce is recognised at all: on a downloaded hose
  holder, 296 of 1130 tree entries were measurement noise and are gone.

Solidon targets the gap between STL repair and learning a full CAD system. The
complete demo is free until 30 October 2026; version 1.0 follows as a one-time
purchase rather than a subscription.

Demo and screenshots: https://solidon3d.de/en/
Detailed changes: https://solidon3d.de/en/changelog.html

I can provide the released 0.3.3 build, the deliberately flawed STL, exact
before-and-after measurements and publication-ready images. There are no
conditions on the verdict.

